3468	{"artifactId":"STORY-c5a4572e3949359a-s1","entry":{"id":"005ee0bf72a04ecaaa63f802327b71e0","actor":{"id":"insrc:approval","kind":"system","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-11-18T08:07:53.822Z","operation":"complete","revision":{"number":1,"contentHash":"7f67311f00c6101c952763f219208b46e606b381c765f6354708dffd623980a3"},"status":{"from":"draft","to":"completed"},"outcome":{"result":"completed","summary":"completed with a code-review override: User approved (in-chat, after insrc_present of BUILD.md + CR.md) overriding the 3 MED changes_requested findings on t1/t3/t6 from the real code review (attempt 2, daemon as reviewer, no technical failures). All three findings cite the same root issue: the LLD/Plan's own 'managed update still uses the signed bridge' acceptance-check wording is factually wrong, discovered during build — a genuinely managed install bypasses the signed bridge entirely via the Node IPC supervisor on every surface (daemon-ctl.sh, VS Code, JetBrains), so no such behavioral fixture is constructible; only the raw installer invoked directly against an unmanaged checkout still legitimately uses the bridge, and that is still tested unchanged. t2/t4/t5 passed the real review cleanly with no findings. t1/t4 also showed scopeRespected:false at insrc_build_step validate, a sequencing artifact of validating all 6 tasks only after implementing all 6 (t2's validate under the same conditions was scope-clean, confirming the mechanism itself works). t3/t5's named tests are JetBrains Kotlin/JUnit files declared unit-level; insrc_build_step[validate] can only execute .test.ts files and accepts reported results only for live/smoke-level tests, so there is no path through that gate for them — verified instead via the project's own jetbrains-plugin/test-bridge/run-focused-junit.ts bridge (all 6 affected Kotlin classes pass), and the same files were reviewed and passed by the real code reviewer in attempt 2.","data":{"codeReviewOverride":"User approved (in-chat, after insrc_present of BUILD.md + CR.md) overriding the 3 MED changes_requested findings on t1/t3/t6 from the real code review (attempt 2, daemon as reviewer, no technical failures). All three findings cite the same root issue: the LLD/Plan's own 'managed update still uses the signed bridge' acceptance-check wording is factually wrong, discovered during build — a genuinely managed install bypasses the signed bridge entirely via the Node IPC supervisor on every surface (daemon-ctl.sh, VS Code, JetBrains), so no such behavioral fixture is constructible; only the raw installer invoked directly against an unmanaged checkout still legitimately uses the bridge, and that is still tested unchanged. t2/t4/t5 passed the real review cleanly with no findings. t1/t4 also showed scopeRespected:false at insrc_build_step validate, a sequencing artifact of validating all 6 tasks only after implementing all 6 (t2's validate under the same conditions was scope-clean, confirming the mechanism itself works). t3/t5's named tests are JetBrains Kotlin/JUnit files declared unit-level; insrc_build_step[validate] can only execute .test.ts files and accepts reported results only for live/smoke-level tests, so there is no path through that gate for them — verified instead via the project's own jetbrains-plugin/test-bridge/run-focused-junit.ts bridge (all 6 affected Kotlin classes pass), and the same files were reviewed and passed by the real code reviewer in attempt 2."}}}}
